scenarios.modelAnswer:
Four boundaries failed, and three of them are in the schema rather than in the model.

First, the recipient is model-controlled. `to` is declared as a plain `string`, so any address is legal, which turns a sentence written in a ticket into an argument the model can pass. An address is an authorization decision, not a parameter. Second, `subject` and `body` are model-authored free text assembled from content the agent read — that is the exfiltration payload, and it is also why the mail looks plausible in the incident review. Third, `attachments` is a list of arbitrary paths read with `fs.readFile` under the process's own rights: it reaches anything the agent can read, including `.env`, other tenants' exports and the CSV in this incident, so the tool has both a data leg and a channel leg at once. Fourth, nothing gated the send and nothing recorded who authorized it, which is why the review took a day.

The rewrite separates drafting from sending and resolves every consequential value server-side:

```ts
// draft: no side effect, safe to run unattended
const draftReply = {
  name: 'draft_reply',
  description: 'Store a reply draft for a ticket; does not send',
  inputSchema: {
    type: 'object',
    properties: {
      ticketId: { type: 'string' },
      templateId: { type: 'string', enum: ['ack', 'resolution', 'need-info'] },
      fields: { type: 'object', properties: { summary: { type: 'string', maxLength: 800 } } },
    },
    required: ['ticketId', 'templateId'],
  },
};

// send: recipient from the ticket, attachments from the document store
async function sendReply({ ticketId, draftId, attachmentIds }: SendArgs) {
  const ticket = await tickets.get(ticketId);        // the actor is already authorized on this ticket
  const draft = await drafts.get(draftId);
  const files = await docs.resolve(attachmentIds);    // ids, not paths: the store enforces the tenant
  const payload = { to: ticket.customer.email, subject: draft.subject, body: draft.body, files };
  await approvals.require(hash(payload));             // human gate bound to the resolved payload
  return mailer.send({ ...payload, idempotencyKey: draftId, tenant: ticket.tenantId });
}
```

The gate: `send_reply` is external and irreversible, so it sits behind a human approval that renders the recipient, subject and attachment names from the resolved payload — never from the model's description of what it is about to send — and the approval is bound to a hash so that editing the payload voids it. Partner domains, if the business genuinely needs them, come from an allowlist maintained by the business, not from ticket text.

The runtime: the mail credential is scoped to one tenant instead of the process's rights, a per-run send cap bounds the damage of a loop, the audit records who approved which payload, and the idempotency key is the draft id so a retry cannot double-send. The sequencing rule matters as much as the tools: once a run has read untrusted ticket text it may draft but not send, so the send is a separate gated step consuming a stored artifact.

Verification is two tests: replay the injected ticket and assert that no mail leaves and only a draft exists, and assert that a traversal path never reaches `fs.readFile`. Nothing in the fix depends on the model refusing, which is the property that makes it hold.

code.code:
async function run(goal: string) {
  const tools = ['shell', 'http', 'write_file'];
  while (true) {
    const action = await model.next(goal, tools); // goal only: no observations, no history
    await runTool(action.name, action.args);      // shell, http, file write, no sandbox
  }
}

scenarios.modelAnswer:
Start with the defect that explains the repetition, because a budget alone would only have stopped a loop that could never finish anyway. `model.next(goal, tools)` is called with the goal and nothing else: the result of every tool call is dropped, so turn 2 sees exactly the input turn 1 saw, re-proposes the same `write_file`, gets the same result, and repeats. The observation step is missing — the result has to be appended to the context tied to the call id, and the next turn reads that state. The loop also has no terminal state: `run` never checks whether the turn was a final answer, it just executes whatever came back, so even a correct answer would be handed to `runTool`.

The rest is the envelope, and it has to be priced rather than asserted. The task is open-ended (the model picks the next action) and the cost of a wrong action is high: `shell` reaches the whole host, `http` is an arbitrary egress channel, and `write_file` has already written outside the working directory. Open-ended plus expensive is the cell where the agent proposes and a human or a deterministic step commits.

The loop, rewritten:

```ts
const budget = { steps: 30, wallClockMs: 5 * 60_000, tokens: 200_000, spendUsd: 1 };
let state = { goal, observations: [], spentUsd: 0 };
const seen = new Set<string>();

for (let step = 0; step < budget.steps; step++) {
  const turn = await model.run(state);                 // full state, not just the goal
  if (turn.final) return { status: 'done', answer: turn.answer };
  const signature = JSON.stringify(turn.toolCall);     // same call twice is a loop, not progress
  if (seen.has(signature)) return { status: 'needs-human', reason: 'repeated-call', state };
  seen.add(signature);
  const result = await executeValidated(turn.toolCall); // allowlist, schema, timeout inside
  state.observations.push({ callId: turn.toolCall.id, result });
}
return { status: 'stopped', reason: 'step-budget', state };
```

Then the controls, in the order they earn their keep. The tool surface is the containment boundary, so `shell` and open `http` leave and become narrow tools — `run_tests`, `format`, `search_docs` — running in an ephemeral container with no network by default, a read-only filesystem and one scratch directory for writes. Writes outside that scratch directory go through an approval, which is also where the outside-the-repo write from last night is blocked. Every run carries step, wall-clock, token and spend budgets plus a cancellation the operator can pull and that reaches the running tool; repeated calls return `needs-human` instead of looping; each effect is logged with the run id and an idempotency key, because a cancelled run may have landed.

Finally, the evidence that would buy more autonomy: a sandbox pass rate on tasks that were not in the prompt examples, a trace where each step's arguments and result are readable, a rollback for the writes, and a bounded blast radius of one account. Until those exist, the honest envelope is propose-and-commit, not a bigger budget.

